WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.176 --> 00:00:02.621
- He was the first African
American billionaire.

00:00:02.621 --> 00:00:05.004
He's a businessman and media magnet,

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:06.675
philanthropist, and investor.

00:00:06.675 --> 00:00:10.040
He's worth over a half a billion dollars.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.990
He's Robert Johnson and here are

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:13.933
his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:15.145
(loudly swishes)

00:00:15.145 --> 00:00:17.068
(loudly thuds)

00:00:17.068 --> 00:00:20.224
- Fundamental connection to doing business

00:00:20.224 --> 00:00:22.480
is basically relationships.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.654
If you really think about it.

00:00:24.654 --> 00:00:29.105
People want to do business
with people they like.

00:00:29.105 --> 00:00:31.550
I mean, every now and then
you'll do a deal with the devil,

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:32.867
but you don't really like the devil,

00:00:32.867 --> 00:00:34.351
but you have to do sometimes.

00:00:34.351 --> 00:00:35.876
But you'd really much rather do

00:00:35.876 --> 00:00:37.172
business with somebody you like,

00:00:37.172 --> 00:00:38.593
somebody who shares your values,

00:00:38.593 --> 00:00:40.975
somebody who you know is committed

00:00:40.975 --> 00:00:42.793
to the same objectives you are,

00:00:42.793 --> 00:00:45.008
somebody who's willing to
work as hard as you are.

00:00:45.008 --> 00:00:47.245
And when you find those people,

00:00:47.245 --> 00:00:49.460
those are people you want
to bring under your tent

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.988
to be part of your business objective,

00:00:51.988 --> 00:00:56.502
and you've got to do that by
sort of presenting yourself

00:00:56.502 --> 00:00:59.637
in a way that people clearly understand

00:00:59.637 --> 00:01:03.023
that you are committed to creating value.

00:01:03.023 --> 00:01:05.447
Business people want to create value.

00:01:05.447 --> 00:01:06.951
It doesn't mean they
don't want to have fun,

00:01:06.951 --> 00:01:10.358
doesn't mean that they
don't want to be involved

00:01:10.358 --> 00:01:11.612
in other kinds of things,

00:01:11.612 --> 00:01:15.227
but if you go to someone and convince them

00:01:15.227 --> 00:01:18.278
that, "I can help you create value."

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:22.959
And by your ability to articulate
that and communicate it,

00:01:22.959 --> 00:01:24.798
and to get people the impression

00:01:24.798 --> 00:01:28.414
that you will work as hard as they will

00:01:28.414 --> 00:01:30.880
to create that value, you're going to find

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.635
people wanting to come to you.

00:01:32.635 --> 00:01:35.582
And so, for me, it's
sort of been like that

00:01:35.582 --> 00:01:37.044
throughout my whole career,

00:01:37.044 --> 00:01:39.627
is being able to say to people,

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.815
and talk to people in a
way that convinces them

00:01:43.815 --> 00:01:46.637
that I am going to do everything I can

00:01:46.637 --> 00:01:50.043
to help make what they
want to achieve a success

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:52.613
and by extension, I'm
going to be a success.

00:01:52.613 --> 00:01:54.196
When I started BET,

00:01:56.772 --> 00:01:59.071
the guy who invested in BET
is a guy named John Maloney,

00:01:59.071 --> 00:02:00.889
he was, at that time, the operator

00:02:00.889 --> 00:02:03.439
of the third largest cable
company in Denver, Colorado,

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:06.803
and he kept investing money into BET

00:02:06.803 --> 00:02:08.412
without taking additional equity.

00:02:08.412 --> 00:02:11.192
And as BET became a big success,

00:02:11.192 --> 00:02:14.619
I ended up with about 75% of the company,

00:02:14.619 --> 00:02:17.168
he ended up with about 25% of the company.

00:02:17.168 --> 00:02:18.401
And one day, I asked him, I said,

00:02:18.401 --> 00:02:21.348
"John, John, you put over
a half a million dollars

00:02:21.348 --> 00:02:23.041
"in BET during the startup,

00:02:23.041 --> 00:02:25.883
"you put it in as debt, you
didn't put it in as equity."

00:02:25.883 --> 00:02:28.537
Which would've give him great ownership.

00:02:28.537 --> 00:02:30.648
I said, "Why'd you do it that way?"

00:02:30.648 --> 00:02:33.678
And he said, "Bob, I always
knew you would work harder

00:02:33.678 --> 00:02:35.454
"for yourself than you would for me."

00:02:35.454 --> 00:02:37.837
And it was that kind of commitment,

00:02:37.837 --> 00:02:40.135
so if you can convey to people

00:02:40.135 --> 00:02:43.834
that you're willing to work hard for them

00:02:43.834 --> 00:02:45.297
and for yourself,

00:02:45.297 --> 00:02:48.695
you've got a business partner,
you're connecting the dots.

00:02:48.695 --> 00:02:52.373
One of the key components
of growing a business

00:02:52.373 --> 00:02:55.508
is getting the necessary capital to grow.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:58.663
So, if you start off were you own 100%,

00:02:58.663 --> 00:03:02.028
but you need more capital
to grow the business,

00:03:02.028 --> 00:03:05.142
you may have to sell off 20%, 30%,

00:03:05.142 --> 00:03:08.339
depending on how you look
at the business needs.

00:03:08.339 --> 00:03:10.199
Don't fear that.

00:03:10.199 --> 00:03:13.146
Don't be afraid about the notion

00:03:13.146 --> 00:03:15.979
that you might fall below 51%

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.770
and therefore, not be minority-owned

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:23.365
because being minority-owned
is the culture you set,

00:03:23.365 --> 00:03:26.750
is the focus you set,
it's the service you make

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:28.694
to serve the minority community,

00:03:28.694 --> 00:03:31.160
and it's your commitment
to do what you can do best

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:32.727
within the minority community.

00:03:32.727 --> 00:03:36.384
Keep in mind, if we go
back to JP Morgan Chase,

00:03:36.384 --> 00:03:38.662
Jamie Dimon probably owns less than

00:03:38.662 --> 00:03:42.027
005% of Chase.
- [Woman] Yeah, he's the CEO.

00:03:42.027 --> 00:03:43.719
- He's the CEO, but no
one will ever question

00:03:43.719 --> 00:03:46.373
whether or not he is charged of Chase.

00:03:46.373 --> 00:03:49.947
But he's brought capital as
a publicly-traded company

00:03:49.947 --> 00:03:51.452
to grow that business.

00:03:51.452 --> 00:03:54.085
So, whether you get money
from outside investors

00:03:54.085 --> 00:03:56.237
or whether you get money
from friends or family,

00:03:56.237 --> 00:03:58.954
don't be afraid to take on capital,

00:03:58.954 --> 00:04:02.569
sell shares or restructure debt or trade

00:04:02.569 --> 00:04:04.805
before there's whatever it is,

00:04:04.805 --> 00:04:08.692
because the notion is
you want to be in charge

00:04:08.692 --> 00:04:11.075
of a growth company, if
that's what you want to be.

00:04:11.075 --> 00:04:12.663
A company that can go to scale,

00:04:12.663 --> 00:04:15.798
a company that competes, and
you need capital to grow.

00:04:15.798 --> 00:04:20.061
If you want to stay
above 51%, by all means.

00:04:20.061 --> 00:04:23.237
If you can do it and
sell equity off, do it.

00:04:23.237 --> 00:04:25.766
But if you have to because you got

00:04:25.766 --> 00:04:28.316
a great strategic partner
that could bring something

00:04:28.316 --> 00:04:30.238
to the table and they want
to be an equity player,

00:04:30.238 --> 00:04:33.770
and you end up with 50 or less than 50,

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:36.466
you're still the CEO, you're
still setting the vision

00:04:36.466 --> 00:04:39.496
for the company, you are
achieving what you set out to do,

00:04:39.496 --> 00:04:41.231
and I think that's very
important to understand

00:04:41.231 --> 00:04:45.138
because we can't get to
scale without growth capital.

00:04:45.138 --> 00:04:47.354
I may not be smart, but I'm not stupid.

00:04:47.354 --> 00:04:49.276
So, I got the heck first.
(laughing)

00:04:49.276 --> 00:04:51.220
And then I said, "John, I
never started a business.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.080
"What advice could you give me?"

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.334
(laughing)

00:04:54.334 --> 00:04:55.755
But I had the check by then.

00:04:55.755 --> 00:04:57.552
(laughing)

00:04:57.552 --> 00:05:00.728
He said, "Bob, get your revenues
up, keep your costs down."

00:05:00.728 --> 00:05:01.731
- [Woman] Okay.

00:05:01.731 --> 00:05:04.762
- That was my 501 business school class.

00:05:04.762 --> 00:05:07.228
And ever since that time,
every business I start,

00:05:07.228 --> 00:05:09.129
revenues up, costs down.

00:05:09.129 --> 00:05:10.906
That's what I do.

00:05:10.906 --> 00:05:12.055
- [Woman] Alright, thank you.

00:05:12.055 --> 00:05:13.271
- Big business is where you
can get your revenues up,

00:05:13.271 --> 00:05:14.964
keep your costs down, and you can find

00:05:14.964 --> 00:05:18.976
a recurring income stream, like
a cable company called BET.

00:05:18.976 --> 00:05:20.272
(laughing)

00:05:20.272 --> 00:05:23.636
The guys pay you, the
cable operators pay you

00:05:23.636 --> 00:05:24.871
for the programming,
the advertisers pay you

00:05:24.871 --> 00:05:25.968
to advertise within the programming,

00:05:25.968 --> 00:05:27.661
and the record companies
give you the videos free,

00:05:27.661 --> 00:05:29.249
you'd have to be pretty dumb

00:05:29.249 --> 00:05:32.238
to lose money on a business like that, so.

00:05:32.238 --> 00:05:35.644
The key to building a relationship

00:05:35.644 --> 00:05:38.152
and so called connecting the dots is

00:05:38.152 --> 00:05:39.659
make your friends before you need 'em.

00:05:39.659 --> 00:05:40.492
- [Man] Right.

00:05:40.492 --> 00:05:43.013
- And that means that first impression,

00:05:43.013 --> 00:05:45.583
that first interaction you have somebody

00:05:45.583 --> 00:05:48.583
is sort of like a stamp of approval.

00:05:50.745 --> 00:05:53.796
And if you do that with
someone and they like you

00:05:53.796 --> 00:05:56.408
and then they in turn tell
somebody else they like you,

00:05:56.408 --> 00:05:58.582
that's where the dots start coming.

00:05:58.582 --> 00:06:03.096
'Cause somebody will say,
"I know Damian, good guy."

00:06:03.096 --> 00:06:04.747
So, I respect the guy who told me that

00:06:04.747 --> 00:06:06.042
so all of a sudden, now,

00:06:06.042 --> 00:06:10.410
he's transferred his stamp of
approval on you over to me.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:12.082
And that's how it happens.

00:06:12.082 --> 00:06:14.088
The one thing I think we have to do

00:06:14.088 --> 00:06:17.285
is we got to smart about consumption.

00:06:17.285 --> 00:06:19.292
We have got to start saving.

00:06:19.292 --> 00:06:22.959
When I went to graduate
school at Princeton,

00:06:24.161 --> 00:06:26.439
and I took one course in economics,

00:06:26.439 --> 00:06:27.985
and there was one turn that I learned.

00:06:27.985 --> 00:06:30.744
It's called, and you guys
should remember this one,

00:06:30.744 --> 00:06:34.275
it's called the marginal
propensity to consume.

00:06:34.275 --> 00:06:35.989
The marginal propensity to consume is

00:06:35.989 --> 00:06:40.608
that you consume way beyond
your margin of wealth

00:06:40.608 --> 00:06:42.718
and you know they call that?

00:06:42.718 --> 00:06:43.972
Debt.

00:06:43.972 --> 00:06:45.686
- [Woman] (lightly laughs) Yeah.

00:06:45.686 --> 00:06:49.853
- We have more debt than
any other group in America,

00:06:51.056 --> 00:06:53.306
Ergo, we have less savings.

00:06:54.714 --> 00:06:57.242
Without savings, you can't invest.

00:06:57.242 --> 00:07:00.168
Without investment, you
can't accumulate wealth.

00:07:00.168 --> 00:07:02.085
We have got to postpone

00:07:03.783 --> 00:07:05.366
sort of what I call

00:07:06.709 --> 00:07:10.042
just unnecessary, impulsive consumption.

00:07:11.975 --> 00:07:14.149
And focus on savings.

00:07:14.149 --> 00:07:16.030
And until we get to that point,

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:19.122
we're not going to have
enough wealth to invest.

00:07:19.122 --> 00:07:22.696
As most of you know,
I've always been vocal

00:07:22.696 --> 00:07:25.496
about economic opportunity
for African Americans.

00:07:25.496 --> 00:07:29.279
I'm what you might call
a serial entrepreneur.

00:07:29.279 --> 00:07:32.623
I get a vision about
something that should be done,

00:07:32.623 --> 00:07:35.486
usually in an area where African Americans

00:07:35.486 --> 00:07:37.722
are economically underrepresented,

00:07:37.722 --> 00:07:41.128
and I try my best to
create a business solution

00:07:41.128 --> 00:07:44.952
to help address these social
and economic problems.

00:07:44.952 --> 00:07:48.275
People here in the same business.

00:07:48.275 --> 00:07:50.762
I mean, if you're
running the same business

00:07:50.762 --> 00:07:53.667
as somebody else, the first
thing I would do is sit down

00:07:53.667 --> 00:07:58.348
and talk about, "Can we
merge and get to scale?"

00:07:58.348 --> 00:08:00.584
That's the first thing I would do.

00:08:00.584 --> 00:08:04.199
There's no way in the world
if I were running a company,

00:08:04.199 --> 00:08:05.850
let's say I was in the security business,

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:08.400
providing security to companies,

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:10.197
and then there's another guy, named Fred

00:08:10.197 --> 00:08:11.367
who had a security company,

00:08:11.367 --> 00:08:14.565
a person named Mary
had a security company.

00:08:14.565 --> 00:08:16.759
Three security companies
all vying for the contracts.

00:08:16.759 --> 00:08:18.995
I would say, "Why don't we merge?

00:08:18.995 --> 00:08:22.328
"Get to scale, reduce our overhead cost,

00:08:23.363 --> 00:08:26.874
"and go out and become the
preeminent security firm

00:08:26.874 --> 00:08:29.507
"in the city of Charlotte."

00:08:29.507 --> 00:08:31.743
There is no way in the
world I would be competing

00:08:31.743 --> 00:08:34.460
against that, that's the
first thing I would do.

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:36.946
You have to believe in yourself.

00:08:36.946 --> 00:08:37.779
- [Woman] Yeah.

00:08:37.779 --> 00:08:39.118
- If you don't believe in yourself,

00:08:39.118 --> 00:08:42.169
if you don't believe that
you can overcome obstacles,

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:44.948
if you don't believe
you have the work ethic,

00:08:44.948 --> 00:08:47.581
if you don't believe you
have the inner confidence

00:08:47.581 --> 00:08:50.925
to project to other
people, it'll never happen.

00:08:50.925 --> 00:08:53.809
Because part of starting a business is

00:08:53.809 --> 00:08:56.902
not only having the vision of an idea,

00:08:56.902 --> 00:09:01.069
you've got to build around
you a team of stakeholders

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.300
who believe in you.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:06.013
People follow leaders.

00:09:06.013 --> 00:09:10.736
People invest in leaders,
people support people

00:09:10.736 --> 00:09:12.972
who have a vision and are willing to,

00:09:12.972 --> 00:09:14.602
and are able to articulate it

00:09:14.602 --> 00:09:16.316
and not just the ability, you know,

00:09:16.316 --> 00:09:18.907
a lot of people got a good gift for gab.

00:09:18.907 --> 00:09:20.809
It's the ability to convince people

00:09:20.809 --> 00:09:23.693
that you're going to be part of something

00:09:23.693 --> 00:09:25.260
that's going to be successful,

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:27.455
you're going to share in the success,

00:09:27.455 --> 00:09:29.461
it's going to be beneficial to you

00:09:29.461 --> 00:09:30.924
and your family in the long run,

00:09:30.924 --> 00:09:33.473
you're going to be respected
for what you contribute,

00:09:33.473 --> 00:09:36.064
and you're going to have an opportunity

00:09:36.064 --> 00:09:39.241
to fulfill your own dreams consistent

00:09:39.241 --> 00:09:41.247
with the dreams of the
person you're working with.

00:09:41.247 --> 00:09:44.027
If you can convey that sense of confidence

00:09:44.027 --> 00:09:46.973
and that sense of leadership,

00:09:46.973 --> 00:09:51.216
and focus on giving people and opportunity

00:09:51.216 --> 00:09:53.556
to grow and be successful with you,

00:09:53.556 --> 00:09:54.998
you're going to have people,

00:09:54.998 --> 00:09:57.046
and I mean really smart people come in

00:09:57.046 --> 00:09:58.634
to be a part of what you do.

00:09:58.634 --> 00:10:01.727
Imagine a group of
people go to a restaurant

00:10:01.727 --> 00:10:03.838
and they start eating.

00:10:03.838 --> 00:10:05.531
Well, they don't have enough
money to pay the bill.

00:10:05.531 --> 00:10:08.101
So, when the waiter comes with the bill,

00:10:08.101 --> 00:10:09.940
they don't want to pay it,

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:11.403
they don't want to make
the sacrifice of paying,

00:10:11.403 --> 00:10:12.991
so they keep ordering.

00:10:12.991 --> 00:10:14.370
And that's what we're doin'.

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:17.442
We just keep piling on debt

00:10:17.442 --> 00:10:19.804
and keep piling on cost

00:10:19.804 --> 00:10:22.960
because we don't want to
make the hard choices.

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:26.793
And look at businesses
who can provide support

00:10:27.745 --> 00:10:30.412
to your business at a cost below

00:10:31.298 --> 00:10:33.638
what you're paying somebody else.

00:10:33.638 --> 00:10:36.971
And if you don't have the money for that

00:10:38.215 --> 00:10:40.117
but it'll help you
jumpstart your business,

00:10:40.117 --> 00:10:42.186
give them equity.

00:10:42.186 --> 00:10:45.279
Trade equity for the service provided.

00:10:45.279 --> 00:10:46.574
It happens all the time.

00:10:46.574 --> 00:10:50.043
When I started BET, I
needed cable distribution.

00:10:50.043 --> 00:10:53.157
Some of the cable at Time
Warner at that time said,

00:10:53.157 --> 00:10:55.999
"Yeah, we'd like to be an
investor, but we also want equity.

00:10:55.999 --> 00:10:57.880
"We'll give you our carry,
but we want equity."

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:01.537
And that's another way
to build a relationship.

00:11:01.537 --> 00:11:03.606
Doesn't take capital, just
you got to be confident,

00:11:03.606 --> 00:11:05.173
but when you provide a service,

00:11:05.173 --> 00:11:07.284
that service is going to
help this business grow,

00:11:07.284 --> 00:11:09.269
you have 10% equity, whatever the trade is

00:11:09.269 --> 00:11:11.004
is going to help you grow.

00:11:11.004 --> 00:11:12.885
- Thank you for watching, I made the video

00:11:12.885 --> 00:11:14.828
because Frank White asked me to.

00:11:14.828 --> 00:11:16.542
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:11:16.542 --> 00:11:17.712
that you want me to profile next,

00:11:17.712 --> 00:11:19.238
leave it in the comments below

00:11:19.238 --> 00:11:20.408
and I'll see what I can do.

00:11:20.408 --> 00:11:23.689
I'd also love to know what
you think about Robert Johnson

00:11:23.689 --> 00:11:28.042
an which of the top 10 lessons
you relate to the most.

00:11:28.042 --> 00:11:29.839
Leave it below, I'm going
to join in the discussion.

00:11:29.839 --> 00:11:31.762
Thank you for watching,
continue to believe,

00:11:31.762 --> 00:11:33.204
and I'll see you soon.

00:11:33.204 --> 00:11:34.479
(loudly swishes)

00:11:34.479 --> 00:11:36.060
(loudly thuds)

